  "prompt_text": "The figure links a charge-density-wave (CDW) transition to changes in Fermi-surface geometry and to the wavevectors expected in STM Fourier maps. In the top row, the electronic structure is compared below and above the CDW temperature. Below the transition, parts of the Fermi surface appear connected by several symmetry-related nesting vectors labeled Q1–Q3, drawn between red hot spots around the zone boundary near M. These connections suggest that scattering between parallel or nearby segments drives the CDW. Above the transition, the same overall hexagonal Brillouin-zone setting remains, but the nesting construction is replaced by small elongated pockets or reconstructed features around the central region, while the main scattering path along the zone edge is still emphasized.\n\nThe lower panel translates these reciprocal-space ideas into an STM Fourier-transform schematic. It shows the parent Bragg peaks of the hexagonal lattice together with additional peaks attributed to CDW order in different alkali compounds. Distinct colored diamonds and circles mark wavevectors associated with Cs, Rb, and K variants, including a lower-symmetry CDW pattern. Overall, the figure conveys that temperature-dependent Fermi-surface topology determines specific CDW modulation vectors, which then appear as characteristic extra spots in STM Fourier space.",
